I still can't agree, because the claim is wrong for inserts. Repeating it from either side won't settle it, so a measurement might.

In PostgreSQL, for example:

```sql
CREATE TABLE t1 (id serial PRIMARY KEY, a int, b int, c text);
CREATE TABLE t2 (LIKE t1 INCLUDING ALL);
CREATE INDEX ON t2(a);
CREATE INDEX ON t2(b);
CREATE INDEX ON t2(c);

\timing on
INSERT INTO t1 (a,b,c) SELECT g, random()*1e6, md5(g::text) FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
INSERT INTO t2 (a,b,c) SELECT g, random()*1e6, md5(g::text) FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
```

The indexed table `t2` will take noticeably longer, because each row also has to be written into three more B-trees.

The only way an index "helps" an insert is by speeding up a check or lookup the insert depends on, such as a foreign key validation, an upsert conflict check, or the SELECT in `INSERT ... SELECT`. Even then, the write itself gets more expensive.

If your benchmark shows indexes making plain inserts faster, I'd like to see the setup. Something else is probably going on, such as caching, a foreign key check, or a different query being timed.

