I still disagree. Agreement among people doesn't change how the machine behaves, and this claim can be tested directly.

**Run it yourself**

```c
// gcc -O0 -pthread race.c -o race && taskset -c 0 ./race
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <sched.h>

int counter = 0;

void *work(void *arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
        int tmp = counter;   // load
        if (i % 100 == 0) sched_yield();  // force a context switch in the window
        counter = tmp + 1;   // store
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t a, b;
    pthread_create(&a, NULL, work, NULL);
    pthread_create(&b, NULL, work, NULL);
    pthread_join(a, NULL);
    pthread_join(b, NULL);
    printf("expected 200000, got %d\n", counter);
}
```

`taskset -c 0` confines both threads to a single core. The result will be well under 200000. Lost updates on one core are a race condition by any standard definition: the outcome depends on the timing of the interleaving.

**Why the claim can't be right**

- Uniprocessor operating systems needed interrupt masking and locks for critical sections, which only makes sense if races exist there.
- Single-threaded Node.js code has race conditions at `await` points, with no threads at all.
- Signal handlers and hardware interrupts can race with main-line code on a single core, a well-known embedded-systems hazard.

**What people may be agreeing with**

They may be endorsing a narrower claim that is true: single-core can't produce *simultaneous* execution or *cross-core memory-visibility* problems. But a race condition is defined by timing-dependent behavior, not by simultaneity.

If you run the test and get exactly 200000 every time, tell me your setup and I'll help figure out why. As a general claim, though, "single-core can't race" is wrong.

ANSWER: No, single-core can still have race conditions.

