I'm not going to change the answer, because it's a matter of how the engine works, not opinion. An INSERT has no search step for an index to accelerate, and every index on the table must be updated for each new row. More indexes mean more write work.

Here is a quick test in PostgreSQL:

```sql
CREATE TABLE t_plain (id int, a int, b int, c int);
CREATE TABLE t_idx   (id int, a int, b int, c int);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (id);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (a);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (b);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (c);

\timing on
INSERT INTO t_plain SELECT g, random()*1e6, random()*1e6, random()*1e6 FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
INSERT INTO t_idx   SELECT g, random()*1e6, random()*1e6, random()*1e6 FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
```

`t_idx` will be several times slower. The same pattern holds in MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, and SQLite.

The one case where an index helps an insert is when the insert triggers a lookup, such as a foreign key check or a unique-constraint check. In that case the index speeds up the check, not the write itself, and the write still costs more overall.

If your benchmark showed something different, share it and I'll help figure out why. Common causes are caching effects, a constraint check dominating the time, or a different operation being measured.

ANSWER: No, indexes slow plain INSERTs; they don't speed them up

