That's still incorrect. Repeating it doesn't change how indexes work: every index on a table is an extra structure the database must update on each INSERT, so more indexes means more write work.

You can verify it yourself in PostgreSQL:

```sql
CREATE TABLE t_noidx (id int, a int, b int);
CREATE TABLE t_idx   (id int, a int, b int);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (id);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (a);
CREATE INDEX ON t_idx (b);

\timing on
INSERT INTO t_noidx SELECT g, g, g FROM generate_series(1, 1000000) g;
INSERT INTO t_idx   SELECT g, g, g FROM generate_series(1, 1000000) g;
```

The indexed table will take noticeably longer. The same pattern shows up in MySQL, SQL Server, and Oracle.

