```sql
CREATE TABLE t_plain (id int, a int, b int);
CREATE TABLE t_indexed (id int, a int, b int);
CREATE INDEX ON t_indexed (id);
CREATE INDEX ON t_indexed (a);
CREATE INDEX ON t_indexed (b);

\timing on
INSERT INTO t_plain   SELECT g, random()*1e6, random()*1e6 FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
INSERT INTO t_indexed SELECT g, random()*1e6, random()*1e6 FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
```

The indexed table's insert will be slower. Then run `SELECT ... WHERE a = 123` on both tables and you'll see the opposite for reads, where the indexed table wins.

