`volatile` gives you visibility and ordering. It does not give you atomicity for read-modify-write operations. `count++` compiles to roughly:

```
getfield count    // volatile read
iconst_1
iadd
putfield count    // volatile write
```

Each volatile access is individually atomic and visible, but nothing stops another thread from running between the read and the write. Two threads can both read 5 and both write 6, so one increment is lost.

You can verify this yourself:

```java
public class Test {
    static volatile int count = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Runnable r = () -> { for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) count++; };
        Thread t1 = new Thread(r), t2 = new Thread(r);
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join(); t2.join();
        System.out.println(count); // almost always < 200000
    }
}
```

If `volatile` made `count++` atomic, this would always print 200000. It doesn't. Replace the field with `AtomicInteger` and `incrementAndGet()` and it will.

This is documented in the Java Language Specification (JLS §17.4 and §17.7) and in *Java Concurrency in Practice*. It is not a matter of opinion.

If you have a specific source or a test showing otherwise, send it and I'll look at it. Without that, my answer stays the same.

ANSWER: No, volatile does not make count++ atomic.

